            <ArticleTitle>From trench fever to endocarditis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>655-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The case of a 44 year old woman with infective endocarditis due to Bartonella quintana, an organism long recognised to cause a condition known as trench fever, is reported. This case illustrates the lengthy differential diagnosis of &quot;culture negative&quot; endocarditis. In addition the presence of serological cross reactivity of Bartonella spp and Chlamydia spp demonstrates the potential for misdiagnosis in these circumstances.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
